ure, having been adapted many times for films, stage, plays, and other forms of art performances, in addition to inspiring the Dorian Awards since 2009.", "newValue": "The Picture of Dorian Gray, published in 1890, is a pivotal work of philosophical fiction and Gothic horror written by Irish author Oscar Wilde. Set against the backdrop of Victorian England, it explores themes of aestheticism, moral duplicity, and the consequences of hedonism. Wilde, a leading figure of the late 19th century literary scene, crafted this narrative during a time of significant social and cultural change, making it a cornerstone of both Gothic literature and the wider canon of English literature. Its enduring relevance reflects the tensions between art and morality, individualism and social responsibility.

The story revolves around Dorian Gray, a young man whose portrait, painted by Basil Hallward, becomes a vessel for his moral decay. After wishing that he could remain eternally young while the portrait ages, Dorian embarks on a life of indulgence and vice, leading to tragic consequences. The novel was first released in a shorter form in Lippincott's Monthly Magazine in July 1890, causing a scandal due to its provocative content. The full version followed in April 1891, further cementing Wilde’s reputation as a daring social commentator and a master of wit and paradox.

